## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code aborts a thread that exceeds a time limit. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken callerToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(callerToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!callerToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | `Task.Run` only checks the token *before* starting. If `work()` is a long-running CPU call that never checks for cancellation, it will run to completion even after timeout. |
| **Uncooperative work won't stop** | If `work` can't be modified to accept a `CancellationToken`, the task keeps running in the background—you just stop *waiting*. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill`. |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`—leaking it causes timer leaks. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary to convert to `TimeoutException`. |

### For Cooperative Work (best-case migration)

If you control the work delegate, pass the token through:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Then inside `work`, call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at regular checkpoints to ensure timely cancellation.

### What Was Removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely—no equivalent needed |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `Task.Wait` / `await` |
